Loupe shows which device on your network talked to which site or service,
how much data moved each way, and when.

It names connections from TLS and QUIC ClientHellos (SNI) and DNS answers,
counts bytes per connection from pf's state table (exact, both directions,
before NAT), and identifies devices from mDNS, DHCP, MAC vendor and the
services they use. Results are kept in SQLite: per minute for 4 hours,
per 5 minutes for 30 days and per hour for a year (the last two adjustable).
Reports live under Reporting, with a dashboard widget.

Plugin Changelog
================

1.3

* Switch map (optional): a tab showing which device is on which port of your managed switches and of the router itself, with Loupe's names and each device's traffic in the period you pick. Needs managed switches with SNMP v2c; the tab appears once one is listed under Settings, Switches
* Switches are read over SNMP when you save one, press Refresh, or open the tab more than 5 minutes after the last read; nothing polls in the background and nothing on a switch is changed
* A quiet device stays on the port it was last found on, marked as such
* Fold a switch's section, or an access point's clients
* A device's page says which switch and port it is on
* A device's page opened from Services or the Switch map leads back there

1.2

Security release with substantial architectural security improvements, following
https://github.com/alokdhir/os-loupe/issues/1

* Packet parsing, the database and reports no longer run as root: they run as a dedicated, unprivileged loupe user
* The daemon opens the capture devices and pf's socket as root, then drops root before it reads a packet
* Reports, which configd starts as root, drop to the loupe user as well
* The loupe user has no supplementary groups, so it can't read OPNsense's configuration
* /var/db/loupe is owned by the loupe user and closed to everyone else
* One small root process remains, to read DHCP lease and static host files that only root can read and pass them to the daemon over a pipe: it parses nothing, takes no input and runs in a Capsicum sandbox that limits it to those files' directories
* Upgrades move existing data to the new user; removing the plugin removes the user and keeps the data

1.1

* Transparent bridges: works with no address on the bridge, counts each connection once, reads names and MAC addresses from forwarded traffic
* Local networks setting

1.0.2

* Settings lists every enabled interface except WAN and bridge members, not only those with a fixed address and no gateway
* Log a warning when a selected interface has no address, since nothing on it can be counted

1.0.1

* Fix a kernel panic (Fatal trap 12 in bpf_filter()) on a LAN that is a plain igc port or similar, not a bridge: the filter that reads packet contents now sees only incoming packets, as FreeBSD's filter can fault on data the router sends with sendfile(), such as the web GUI. The FreeBSD bug itself: https://bugs.freebsd.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=300493

